ve system that we have  there are huge rewards for showing that  something is completely different so  trying to build a new branch in a  completely different direction instead  of an empty void that was never there  before and the the interesting thing is  how difficult it is to do that and you  to convince everybody because you start  growing a little sapling in some  particular area and if it really isn't  robust it just falls off it withers away  but that's how science sort of works is  that some of these ideas actually are  correct and then we build on them and  the whole enterprise incorporates that  into the fabric so I think that the  strong thing about a disease it evolves  it's not completely static it does  evolve and for that that you know this  this web of really strong results is one  of the strong things about science was  what's in good shape and yet at the same  time the competition encourages people  to find something different something  new that will gain them huge notoriety  and and therefore success but they have  to convince everybody else that that's  correct so um that the tension between  those sides is partly good what we might  ask is of the money of the huge amount  of resources we put into science are we  using it most efficiently to find those  new places yet that always seems to be I  mean here in the United States as I'm  sure you're aware there is a political  movement which likes to focus on such  financial subjects as waste and debt and  government spending and it's become very  very popular to find very particular  scientific projects that look at you  know the mating rituals of the manatee  only during April of odd numbered years  right I mean of course that's not a real  one but it's obviously it's always  caricatured right but my favorite  example is actually showing does  moonlight make plants grow very small  about these are presented as sort of  examples of where science is doing  something that's of no purpose or use or  s